#: src/std-types/option.md:31
msgid "`Option` is widely used, not just in the standard library."
msgstr ""

#: src/std-types/option.md:32
msgid ""
"`unwrap` will return the value in an `Option`, or panic. `expect` is similar "
"but takes an error message."
msgstr ""

#: src/std-types/option.md:34
msgid ""
"You can panic on None, but you can't \"accidentally\" forget to check for "
"None."
msgstr ""

#: src/std-types/option.md:36
msgid ""
"It's common to `unwrap`/`expect` all over the place when hacking something "
"together, but production code typically handles `None` in a nicer fashion."
msgstr ""

#: src/std-types/option.md:39
msgid ""
"The \"niche optimization\" means that `Option<T>` typically has the same "
"size in memory as `T`, if there is some representation that is not a valid "
"value of T. For example, a reference cannot be NULL, so `Option<&T>` "
"automatically uses NULL to represent the `None` variant, and thus can be "
"stored in the same memory as `&T`."
msgstr ""
